// Practice quiz: Using Copilot and chat assistants to explain, not answer
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/methods-and-recursion/ai-assistants-explain-not-answer

::o1 explain an exception::Your program stops with a NullPointerException on line 12. Which request to an assistant keeps the learning with you? {
=Explain what this NullPointerException means and which variable on line 12 could be null. Do not write the fixed code.#Correct. It names the exact problem, asks for an explanation and a location, and leaves the fix to you.
~Fix this.#Not quite. That asks for the finished answer, so you may get working code without learning what was wrong.
~Rewrite my whole class so it works.#Not quite. That hands the whole task to the assistant.
~Does my code work? Answer yes or no.#Not quite. A yes or no gives you nothing to learn from or check.
}

::o1 parts of a request::What should an explain-not-answer request include? {
=The exact error or code, a request for an explanation, and a note that you will write the fix yourself#Correct. Naming the exact problem gets a focused explanation, and the note keeps the solution yours.
~The full assignment text, so the assistant has every detail#Not quite. That invites the assistant to solve the whole assignment.
~A request for finished code with comments#Not quite. Comments do not change that the assistant wrote the solution.
~As little detail as possible, so the assistant can be creative#Not quite. A vague request gets a vague or wrong explanation.
}

::o2 checking a claim::An assistant says you read a String's length with s.length and no parentheses. How do you check this? {
=Read the String class in the official API documentation, or compile the smallest example that uses it#Correct. An explanation is a claim; documentation or a small experiment settles it.
~Ask the same assistant whether it is sure#Not quite. The assistant can repeat a confident mistake; it does not check itself.
~Accept it because the explanation is detailed#Not quite. Detail and confidence do not make a claim correct.
~Look for a forum post that agrees#Not quite. Agreement is not evidence; the documentation or a run is.
}

::o2 when they disagree::The output of your small experiment contradicts the assistant's explanation. What do you do? {
=Trust the observed output, confirm it in the documentation if needed, and record that the claim was contradicted#Correct. Running the code shows what Java actually does, which is the point of the check.
~Trust the assistant, because it has read more code than you#Not quite. The program's real behavior wins over any explanation of it.
~Change the experiment until it matches the explanation#Not quite. That hides the evidence instead of learning from it.
~Delete the experiment and move on#Not quite. The contradiction is worth recording, because it is what you verified.
}

::o3 a useful record::Which entry is a useful record of getting help from an assistant? {
=What you asked, a short summary of what the assistant produced, and what you verified, how and with what result#Correct. Those three parts show which ideas came from the tool and which you confirmed yourself.
~The full solution the assistant wrote, pasted in#Not quite. A record summarizes what was produced; it never pastes a finished solution.
~The words used AI#Not quite. That does not say what was asked, produced or checked.
~Only your final code#Not quite. Final code alone does not show what came from the tool or what you verified.
}

::o3 why keep a record::Why keep a record of what an assistant produced and what you verified? {
=It shows your instructor, and later you, which ideas came from the tool and which you confirmed yourself#Correct. The record separates the assistant's claims from your own checked understanding.
~So you can hand in the assistant's code as your own work#Not quite. The record does the opposite: it makes the tool's part visible.
~Because assistants stop working without one#Not quite. The record is for you and your instructor, not for the tool.
~So the assistant remembers your earlier questions#Not quite. The record is kept by you, for people to read.
}
